#---
# purpose: "a closure chosen by a runtime branch reaches the forwarder as a union of two constructions, and the word it carries decides which body runs"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.127, the dynamic half. No key can pin which closure `f` is: the
# `case` scrutinee comes back out of the mailbox, so `f` is typed as the union
# `#lambda(int) | #lambda(float)` and the only thing that can separate the two
# is the word the mint stamped on the value.
#
# `P.run/2` and `P.twice/2` are each ONE body here, taking that union, and the
# call to `f` is indirect: the value's own word selects the construction
# wrapper, whose members then answer for the capture layout they were built at.
# So this program asks no callable question at a callsite -- the word answers
# on its own -- and its plan is the same one it had before the callable axis
# named constructions.
#
# The twin `same_lambda_two_capture_types` is the static shape of the same
# defect, and it is the one that needs the question: there the forwarder is
# shared by two callers whose consumers differ, so a callsite must ask.
defmodule P do
  fn twice(f, x), do: f.(f.(x))
  fn run(f, x), do: twice(f, x)
  fn mk(c), do: fn (n) -> n + c end
end

fn main() do
  send(self(), 1)
  n = receive do v -> v end
  f = case n > 0 do
    true -> P.mk(1)
    _ -> P.mk(0.5)
  end
  dbg(P.run(f, 10))
  g = case n < 0 do
    true -> P.mk(1)
    _ -> P.mk(0.5)
  end
  dbg(P.run(g, 10))
end
